Although ChatGPT has garnered immense popularity, it lacks reliable factual accuracy. While recent attempts aim to flag factually incorrect outputs, they remain experimental. We take a different path by leveraging the factuality of LLM responses as signals for their underlying strengths and limitations. Specifically, we employ ChatGPT-4 to label MiniLLaMAs for medical question answering (MQA), focusing on factual accuracy. We then develop data-efficient specialized models by optimizing these labeled datasets for both helpfulness and factual accuracy. Our findings indicate that while specialized models significantly boost factual accuracy, factualness alone is an insufficient signal for specialization. Furthermore, we observe that LLMs tend to overestimate the factual accuracy of their outputs. By analyzing evaluation metrics and adversarial examples, we identify that effective specialization requires a multi-dimensional signal that balances factual accuracy with helpfulness. To provide such models for clinical support, it is essential to manage risks associated with hallucinations and bias. We propose that adversarial testing for bias and fairness, combined with factuality-aware fine-tuning, are critical steps toward ensuring robust and safe LLM performance in medical question answering.

